use std
into sevenz.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace sevenz). It reads 7z archives held in memory: several can be open at once, each named by an integer handle, like the zip module. It reads plain and encoded (compressed) headers, solid and non-solid folders, and decodes the coders Copy, LZMA, LZMA2, Deflate, BZip2, Delta and BCJ x86; a folder is decoded once and its files are sliced out of it. Files that need an AES coder are reported as encrypted and never decoded; any other coder (PPMd, BCJ2, ARM64, Deflate64 and so on) makes the read of the files that need it fail with a warning. Every module-level name starts with `sz_` or `SZ_`. Problems are reported with `sz_warn`, which uses `dm::dm_warn_add`, and a function that fails leaves its output list empty unless said otherwise.

```amni
import dm;
import pack;
import lzma;
```

Other modules used (exactly as they behave): `lzma::lz_le(s const byte[:], p int, n int) int` reads an unsigned little-endian number of n bytes (1 to 8) at offset p of s, or -1 when it does not fit inside s or an 8-byte value is 2 to the power 63 or more. `lzma::lz_lzma_dec(src const byte[:], props const byte[:], size int, out byte[>..]&) bool` decodes a raw LZMA stream and appends exactly size bytes to out (props is the 5 coder property bytes); it returns false on failure with out cut back to its earlier length. `lzma::lz_lzma2_dec(src const byte[:], prop int, out byte[>..]&) bool` decodes a complete LZMA2 stream and appends it to out (prop is the coder's one property byte); on failure it returns false with out cut back. `lzma::lz_x86(buf byte[>..]&, start int, ip int)` undoes the BCJ x86 filter in place on buf from index start to its end, ip being 0. `lzma::lz_delta(buf byte[>..]&, start int, dist int)` undoes the Delta filter in place on buf from index start to its end. `pack::pk_crc32(s const byte[:], crc int) int` continues a CRC-32 over s (call it with crc 0 to start; the result is from 0 to 4294967295). `pack::pk_inflate(src const byte[:], start int, out byte[>..]&, endp int&) bool` decodes one raw DEFLATE stream that begins at byte start of src, appends the result to out, sets endp to the offset after the stream and returns false when the data is damaged or too long. `pack::pk_bunzip(data const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out and decodes bzip2 data, returning false when it is damaged. `pack::pk_norm_eq(a const byte[:], b const byte[:], ci bool) bool` compares two names after dropping leading "/" and "./" prefixes and trailing "/" and turning backslashes into slashes, with ASCII case ignored when ci is true. `dm::dm_utf16(s const byte[:], big bool, out byte[>..]&)` appends to out the UTF-8 of the UTF-16 bytes s (little-endian when big is false). `dm::dm_warn_add(msg const byte[:])` adds one warning line.

`var sz_cap = 268435456` is the most bytes one decoded folder or coder output may have (256 MiB); the tests lower it. `var sz_said = false` is set when `sz_warn` was called since the current open or read started. `sz_warn(msg const byte[:])` sets `sz_said` to true and calls `dm::dm_warn_add(msg)`. `sz_over() bool` calls `sz_warn("decompressed data passes the limit of 256 MiB")` and returns false.

`struct SzArc` has `base int` (where the archive's bytes start in `sz_data`), `len int`, `e0 int` (the index of its first entry in `sz_ents`), `n0 int` (where its names start in `sz_names`), `f0 int` (its first folder in `sz_folds`), `c0 int` (its first coder in `sz_coders`), `p0 int` (its first pack stream in `sz_packs`), `o0 int` (where its coder orders start in `sz_ord`), `r0 int` (where its coder properties start in `sz_props`), `cnt int` (the entry count) and `live bool`.

`struct SzCoder` has `id int` (the method id bytes read as one big-endian number), `nin int`, `nout int`, `pat int` and `plen int` (the coder's property bytes in `sz_props`) and `usize int` (the size of its output, -1 until known). `struct SzFold` has `arc int` (the handle of its archive), `c0 int` and `nc int` (its coders in `sz_coders`), `o0 int` and `no int` (the decoding order of its coders in `sz_ord`: coder numbers local to the folder, first the one that reads the pack stream and last the one that makes the final data; `no` is 0 when the coders do not form one simple chain), `pk int` and `npk int` (its pack streams in `sz_packs`), `main int` (the local number of the coder whose output is the final data, -1 when unknown), `usize int` (the size of the final data, -1 when unknown) and `crc int` (the CRC-32 of the final data, -1 when none). `struct SzPack` has `off int` (the offset of the pack stream from the start of its archive) and `size int`. `struct SzEnt` has `nat int` and `nlen int` (the UTF-8 name in `sz_names`), `size int`, `dir bool`, `fold int` (the folder in `sz_folds` that holds its data, -1 for none), `off int` (where its data starts inside the decoded folder), `crc int` (-1 when none) and `enc bool` (its folder uses an AES coder).

Module state: `var sz_data byte[>..]` (the bytes of every open archive one after another), `var sz_names byte[>..]`, `var sz_arcs SzArc[>..]`, `var sz_ents SzEnt[>..]`, `var sz_folds SzFold[>..]`, `var sz_coders SzCoder[>..]`, `var sz_props byte[>..]`, `var sz_ord int[>..]` and `var sz_packs SzPack[>..]`. A handle is an index into `sz_arcs`. Header reading state: `var sz_hb byte[>..]` (the header bytes being read), `var sz_hp = 0` (the next byte of it) and `var sz_hbad = false` (set when a read passed the end or a value was not valid). Scratch lists used while a header is read: `var sz_ssz int[>..]`, `var sz_scr int[>..]`, `var sz_sfo int[>..]` and `var sz_sof int[>..]` (for every substream in order: its size, its CRC or -1, its folder and its offset inside that folder), `var sz_bi int[>..]` and `var sz_bo int[>..]` (the bind pairs of the folder being read), `var sz_tmp int[>..]`, `var sz_pk0 = 0` (the index in `sz_packs` of the first pack stream of the streams info being read), `var sz_fo0 = 0` (the index in `sz_folds` of its first folder) and `var sz_hsub = false` (a substreams section was read). Decoded folder cache: `var sz_cf = -1` (the index of the folder whose decoded data is in `sz_cbuf`, -1 for none) and `var sz_cbuf byte[>..]`.

`sz_hbyte() int` returns the byte of `sz_hb` at `sz_hp` and advances `sz_hp`; when `sz_hp` is at or past the end it sets `sz_hbad` and returns 0.

`sz_hnum() int` reads a 7z number. first is the next byte (`sz_hbyte`), mask starts at 0x80 and the value at 0. For i from 0 to 7: when (first and mask) is 0, the number is the value plus ((first and (mask minus 1)) shifted left by 8 times i), and it is returned; otherwise the value gets the next byte (`sz_hbyte`) shifted left by 8 times i or-ed in, and mask is shifted right by 1. When all 8 rounds took a byte the value is returned. It sets `sz_hbad` and returns 0 when a byte was missing or when the number is 2 to the power 48 or more (nothing in a header is that large).

`sz_hu32() int` reads 4 bytes little-endian (`sz_hbyte` four times, lowest first). `sz_hskip(n int)` advances `sz_hp` by n, or sets `sz_hbad` when n is negative or passes the end of `sz_hb`.

`sz_hvec(n int, v int[>..]&)` reads a bit vector of n bits: it clears v, sets `sz_hbad` (leaving v empty) when n is negative or the (n plus 7) divided by 8 bytes are not all there, otherwise pushes n entries, entry i being 1 when bit (7 minus i modulo 8) of the byte at `sz_hp` plus i divided by 8 is set and 0 otherwise, and advances `sz_hp` by those bytes.

`sz_hdig(n int, v int[>..]&)` reads a digest list for n items and sets v to n entries, each the item's CRC or -1 when it has none: the first byte says "all defined" when it is not 0; otherwise a bit vector of n flags follows (`sz_hvec` into `sz_tmp`); then for every defined item a 4-byte CRC (`sz_hu32`) follows in order.

`sz_packinfo(a int) bool` reads a pack info section (the id byte was already read): `sz_pk0` becomes the length of `sz_packs`; pos is `sz_hnum` and n is `sz_hnum` (set `sz_hbad` through a failure when n is more than the header bytes left). Then property ids are read with `sz_hnum` until id 0 (end): id 9 reads n sizes with `sz_hnum` and pushes for each a `SzPack` with off equal to 32 plus pos plus the sizes of the earlier ones and that size; id 10 reads a digest list of n items with `sz_hdig` into `sz_tmp` and ignores it; any other id fails. Fail when `sz_hbad` is set, when fewer than n pack streams were pushed, or when a pack stream's off plus size passes the length of archive a. Return true.

`sz_folder(pk int&) bool` reads one folder description and pushes its `SzFold` (and its coders and properties and order) and returns false on any problem. nc is `sz_hnum` (it must be from 1 to 32). For each coder: flags is `sz_hbyte`; fail when bit 0x80 of flags is set; the id size is the low 4 bits of flags and must be at most 8; the id is that many bytes read big-endian; nin and nout are 1, but when bit 0x10 is set they are `sz_hnum` and `sz_hnum` (each must be at most 32); when bit 0x20 is set the property size is `sz_hnum` and that many bytes are appended to `sz_props` (check they exist and advance), otherwise there are no properties; push a `SzCoder` with usize -1. Let tin be the sum of the nin and tout the sum of the nout; the number of bind pairs is tout minus 1 (fail when that is negative); for each bind pair read an in index and an out index with `sz_hnum` into `sz_bi` and `sz_bo` (cleared first; each must be below tin and tout respectively). The number of pack streams is tin minus the bind pair count (fail when below 1). When it is more than 1, that many in-stream indices follow (`sz_hnum`) and are skipped; when it is exactly 1 nothing follows. The folder is simple when every coder has nin 1 and nout 1 and the pack stream count is 1: then every coder number equals both its in index and its out index. For a simple folder the main coder is the coder number that is not in `sz_bo`; build the order by starting with that coder, then repeating: look for a bind pair whose in index equals the current coder number; when there is none stop, otherwise the next coder is that pair's out index (stop with a failure when the list grows beyond nc); when the list ended up with nc coders, push them into `sz_ord` in reverse (the last one found first). When the folder is not simple, or the chain was incomplete, its order stays empty (`no` is 0), and main is -1 unless tout equals nc and some coder number is not in `sz_bo`, in which case main is that number. Push a `SzFold` with `arc` set to `sz_arcs.len` minus 1, c0 the index of its first coder in `sz_coders`, o0 the index of its first order entry in `sz_ord`, pk equal to the current value of pk, npk, usize -1 and crc -1; then add npk to pk. Return false when `sz_hbad` is set.

`sz_unpackinfo(a int) bool` reads an unpack info section (the id byte was already read): the next number must be 11 (folders), then nf is `sz_hnum` (it must not be more than the header bytes left) and a byte that must be 0 (no external data). Set pk to `sz_pk0` and call `sz_folder(pk)` nf times (fail when it fails). Then the next number must be 12 (unpack sizes): for every folder pushed by this section, for each of its coders, nout numbers are read with `sz_hnum` and the first of them becomes the coder's `usize`. Then read ids with `sz_hnum` until 0 (end): id 10 reads a digest list of nf items with `sz_hdig` into `sz_tmp` and stores each CRC into the `crc` of the matching folder; any other id fails. At the end each folder's `usize` is the `usize` of its main coder when main is 0 or more (the coder with that local number, that is `sz_coders` at c0 plus main). Fail when `sz_hbad` is set.

`sz_substreams() bool` reads a substreams section (the id byte was already read) for the folders from `sz_fo0` on, and fills `sz_ssz`, `sz_scr`, `sz_sfo` and `sz_sof`, which it clears first, and sets `sz_hsub` to true. The per-folder substream counts start at 1 each. id is `sz_hnum`; when it is 13 the counts are read (one `sz_hnum` per folder, each at most the header bytes left plus 1) and id is read again. When id is 9, for every folder with a count above 0: its first count minus 1 sizes are read with `sz_hnum`, and its last size is the folder's `usize` minus their sum (fail when that is negative); then id is read again. When id is not 9 every folder with a count of 1 gets its `usize` as the size, and a count above 1 fails. Every substream's offset is the sum of the sizes before it in its folder. The CRCs: the number of items that need a CRC is the sum, over the folders, of the count, except that a folder with count 1 and a known `crc` counts 0 (it uses the folder's CRC). While id is not 0: id 10 reads a digest list of that many items with `sz_hdig` into `sz_tmp`, and anything else fails; id is read again. Then `sz_scr` gets for every substream the folder's `crc` when its folder has count 1 and a known `crc`, else the next entry of the digest list (or -1 when no digest list was read). Fail when `sz_hbad` is set.

`sz_streams(a int) bool` reads a streams info section (the id byte was already read) for archive a: it sets `sz_pk0` to the length of `sz_packs`, `sz_fo0` to the length of `sz_folds`, clears `sz_ssz`, `sz_scr`, `sz_sfo` and `sz_sof` and sets `sz_hsub` to false, then reads ids with `sz_hnum` until 0: id 6 calls `sz_packinfo(a)`, id 7 calls `sz_unpackinfo(a)`, id 8 calls `sz_substreams()`, any other id fails; a call that fails makes this fail. Afterwards when `sz_hsub` is false every folder from `sz_fo0` on gets one substream of its `usize` (fail when that is -1) with the folder's `crc`, folder number and offset 0. Return false when `sz_hbad` is set.

`sz_enc_fold(f int) bool` is true when any coder of folder f (the `nc` coders from `c0` in `sz_coders`) has the id 0x06F10701 (AES).

`sz_files(a int) bool` reads a files info section (the id byte was already read) and pushes the `SzEnt` entries of archive a. n is `sz_hnum`; fail when n is more than 4000000 or more than the header bytes left. Lists es and ef (bit vectors, empty to start) and the name positions are kept. Then properties are read until id 0: id is `sz_hnum`, size is `sz_hnum`, end is `sz_hp` plus size (fail when it passes the end); id 0x0E reads `sz_hvec(n, es)`; id 0x0F reads `sz_hvec(ne, ef)` where ne is the number of ones in es; id 0x10 reads `sz_hvec(ne, tmp)` into a scratch list; id 0x11 reads the names: a byte that must be 0, then for each of the n files a name made of 2-byte little-endian units up to and including a unit of 0 (fail when the data of the property ends first); the name is the units before the 0 unit, converted with `dm::dm_utf16(units, false, `sz_names`)` appended to `sz_names` and remembered as its start and length in `sz_names` (the start is the length of `sz_names` just before the conversion); ids 0x12, 0x13, 0x14 and 0x18 (times and start positions) and 0x15 (attributes) read a byte all, then when all is 0 a bit vector `sz_hvec(n, tmp)` and count its ones as d, otherwise d is n; then a byte that must be 0 (fail otherwise), then 8 bytes per defined item for ids 0x12, 0x13, 0x14 and 0x18 and 4 bytes per defined item for id 0x15 are skipped (`sz_hp` grows by d times that, fail when that passes end); id 0x19 (padding) needs all of its size bytes to be 0 (fail otherwise); every other id is skipped. For ids 0x0E, 0x0F, 0x10, 0x11, 0x12, 0x13, 0x14, 0x15, 0x18 and 0x19 fail when `sz_hp` is not exactly end after the property was read. After each property `sz_hp` is set to end. Fail when `sz_hbad` is set. When no names property was read every name is empty (start 0, length 0). Then for i from 0 to n minus 1 push the entry: when es has 1 at i the file has no stream: its size is 0, its folder -1, its off 0, its crc -1, `enc` false, and it is a directory unless ef has a 1 at the position of this empty stream among the empty streams (the ones before it counted in es); otherwise it takes the next substream (in order from `sz_ssz`; fail when there are none left): size from `sz_ssz`, crc from `sz_scr`, folder from `sz_sfo`, off from `sz_sof`, dir false and `enc` from `sz_enc_fold` of its folder. The name fields are the remembered start and length. After the last entry fail when any substream of `sz_ssz` was not taken. Return true.

`sz_header(a int) bool` reads a header (the leading byte 1 was already read) of archive a: first `sz_ssz`, `sz_scr`, `sz_sfo` and `sz_sof` are cleared (so an archive without a streams info has no substreams left over from an earlier one) and `sz_hsub` is set to false; then id is `sz_hnum`; when it is 2 the archive properties are skipped (read property ids with `sz_hnum` until 0, each followed by a size from `sz_hnum` and that many bytes skipped) and id is read again; id 3 (additional streams) fails; when id is 4 call `sz_streams(a)` (fail when it fails) and read id again; when id is 5 call `sz_files(a)` (fail when it fails) and then read one more id with `sz_hnum` whose value is not checked (the real 7-Zip tool ignores it); when id was not 5 it must now be 0. Return false when `sz_hbad` is set or when `sz_hp` is not equal to the length of `sz_hb` (every header byte must have been used).

`sz_unsup(id int)` adds, through `sz_warn`, the warning "7z: unsupported method " followed by the name "PPMd" for the id 0x030401, "BCJ2" for 0x0303011B, "ARM64" for 0x0A, "Deflate64" for 0x040109, and for any other id its hexadecimal digits in upper case (at least 2 digits, with one leading 0 added when the number of digits would be odd, so the id 0x03030501 is written 03030501).

`sz_coder(c int, src const byte[:], dst byte[>..]&) bool` runs the decoding coder number c (an index into `sz_coders`) on src and appends the result to dst (an empty list); it fails otherwise. u is the coder's `usize`: fail through `sz_over()` when u is more than `sz_cap`, and fail when u is negative. By id: 0 (Copy) appends src; 0x21 (LZMA2) needs a property size of 1 and calls `lzma::lz_lzma2_dec(src, that byte, dst)`; 0x030101 (LZMA) needs property size of at least 5 and calls `lzma::lz_lzma_dec(src, the first 5 property bytes, u, dst)`; 0x040108 (Deflate) calls `pack::pk_inflate(src, 0, dst, e)`; 0x040202 (BZip2) calls `pack::pk_bunzip(src, dst)`; any other id calls `sz_unsup` and fails. The result must be exactly u bytes long and, for LZMA and LZMA2, `lzma::lz_used` must equal the length of src (every packed byte is used), otherwise fail with the warning "7z: corrupt or truncated data" through `sz_warn`.

`sz_filter(c int, buf byte[>..]&) bool` applies the filter coder number c in place on buf (all of it): id 3 (Delta) calls `lzma::lz_delta(buf, 0, d)` where d is 1 plus the first property byte (1 when it has no properties); id 4 or 0x03030103 (BCJ x86) calls `lzma::lz_x86(buf, 0, 0)`; both return true. `sz_is_filter(id int) bool` is true for the ids 3, 4 and 0x03030103.

`sz_fold_dec(f int, out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out and decodes folder number f of `sz_folds` into it. It fails (adding the warning through `sz_warn`, "7z: encrypted data" when `sz_enc_fold(f)` holds; when the folder's `no` is 0, `sz_unsup` of the id of the first coder of the folder (in the order of `sz_coders`) whose id is none of 0, 3, 4, 0x03030103, 0x21, 0x030101, 0x040108 and 0x040202, or the warning "7z: unsupported coder layout" when there is no such coder; and "7z: corrupt or truncated data" for a pack stream that does not lie inside its archive) when needed, and fails through `sz_over()` when the folder's `usize` is more than `sz_cap`. The pack stream is `sz_packs` at the folder's pk; its bytes are the slice of `sz_data` from the archive's base plus off for size bytes. Use two scratch lists x and y and a number cur: 0 means the data so far is the pack stream slice, 1 means it is in x, 2 that it is in y. For each of the folder's `no` coders in order (coder c is `sz_coders` at the folder's c0 plus `sz_ord` at the folder's o0 plus the step): when its id is a filter (`sz_is_filter`): when cur is 0 append the pack slice to x and set cur to 1; call `sz_filter(c, the list holding the data)` and fail when it fails. Otherwise: the source is the pack slice when cur is 0, x when cur is 1 and y when cur is 2; the destination is x when cur is 0 or 2 and y when cur is 1 (cleared first); call `sz_coder(c, source, destination)` (fail when it fails); clear the source list when it was one; cur becomes the destination's number. At the end append the list holding the data to out. When the folder's `crc` is not -1 and `pack::pk_crc32(out, 0)` differs, clear out, add the warning "7z: CRC mismatch" through `sz_warn` and fail. Any failure clears out, and a failure without a specific warning adds "7z: corrupt or truncated data" through `sz_warn`.

`sz_undo(ra int, re int, rn int, rf int, rc int, rp int, ro int, rr int, rd int)` cuts the module lists back to the lengths given: `sz_arcs` to ra, `sz_ents` to re, `sz_names` to rn, `sz_folds` to rf, `sz_coders` to rc, `sz_packs` to rp, `sz_ord` to ro, `sz_props` to rr and `sz_data` to rd; and when `sz_cf` is rf or more it sets `sz_cf` to -1 and clears `sz_cbuf`.

`sz_open(data const byte[:]) int` returns a handle of 0 or more, -1 when data is not a usable 7z archive and -2 when the archive's header is encrypted. Not 7z (-1, no state changed): fewer than 32 bytes, or the first 6 bytes are not 0x37 0x7A 0xBC 0xAF 0x27 0x1C, or the byte at 6 (the major version) is not 0. Damaged (-1, with the warning "7z: damaged archive" through `sz_warn`, no state changed): the start header's CRC (4 bytes little-endian at 8, over the 20 bytes at 12) is wrong, or noff (8 bytes at 12, via `lzma::lz_le`) or nsize (8 bytes at 20) is negative or more than the length of data, or 32 plus noff plus nsize passes the length of data, or nsize is above 0 and `pack::pk_crc32` of the header bytes at 32 plus noff differs from the 4-byte number at 28. Set `sz_said` to false and remember the current lengths of all the module lists (for `sz_undo`). Append data to `sz_data` after recording the base and push a `SzArc` (e0, n0, f0, c0, p0, o0 and r0 being the current lengths of `sz_ents`, `sz_names`, `sz_folds`, `sz_coders`, `sz_packs`, `sz_ord` and `sz_props`, cnt 0, live true); a is its index. When nsize is 0 the archive is a valid empty one: return a. Otherwise set `sz_hb` to a copy of the header bytes (clear it, then append the slice of data at 32 plus noff for nsize bytes). Then repeat at most 4 times: `sz_hp` is 0, `sz_hbad` is false; id is `sz_hbyte`. When id is 0x17 (an encoded header): remember the lengths of `sz_folds`, `sz_coders`, `sz_packs`, `sz_ord` and `sz_props`, call `sz_streams(a)` (on failure undo with `sz_undo` and return -1), take the first folder of that section (fail the same way when there is none); when `sz_enc_fold` holds for it undo with `sz_undo` and return -2; decode it with `sz_fold_dec` into a scratch list (undo and return -1 on failure), cut `sz_folds`, `sz_coders`, `sz_packs`, `sz_ord` and `sz_props` back to the remembered lengths, make `sz_hb` a copy of the scratch list and repeat. When id is 1: call `sz_header(a)`; on failure undo and return -1; otherwise leave the loop. Any other id undoes and returns -1 (and so does leaving the loop after 4 rounds). On success set the archive's cnt to `sz_ents` length minus its e0 and return a. Every failure path also adds the warning "7z: damaged archive" through `sz_warn` unless `sz_said` is already true.

`sz_count(h int) int` is the entry count of handle h, or 0 for a bad or closed handle. `sz_name(h int, i int) const byte[:]` is the UTF-8 name of entry i of handle h (a slice of `sz_names`; directories have no trailing slash), empty for a bad handle or index. `sz_size(h int, i int) int` is the size of entry i (0 for directories and empty files), -1 for a bad handle or index. `sz_is_dir(h int, i int) bool` is the entry's `dir`, `sz_encrypted(h int, i int) bool` its `enc`; both are false for a bad handle or index. Entry i of handle h is `sz_ents` at the archive's e0 plus i, valid when h is a live handle and i is from 0 to its cnt minus 1.

`sz_find(h int, name const byte[:]) int` returns the index of the first entry of handle h whose name equals name byte for byte; failing that the first one for which `pack::pk_norm_eq(entry name, name, false)` holds; failing that the first one for which `pack::pk_norm_eq(entry name, name, true)` holds; -1 when none or the handle is bad.

`sz_read(h int, i int, out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out and appends the contents of entry i of handle h. It returns false (leaving out empty) for a bad handle or index; for an entry without a stream (a directory or empty file) it returns true with out empty; when the entry is encrypted it adds the warning "7z: encrypted entry" through `sz_warn` and returns false. Otherwise the decoded data of its folder is needed: when `sz_cf` is not the entry's folder, decode the folder into `sz_cbuf` with `sz_fold_dec` (on failure return false and leave `sz_cf` at -1) and set `sz_cf` to the folder; the entry's data are the size bytes of `sz_cbuf` starting at its off (fail with the warning "7z: corrupt or truncated data" when they pass the end). Append them to out; when the entry's crc is not -1 and `pack::pk_crc32(out, 0)` differs, clear out, add the warning "7z: CRC mismatch" and return false.

`sz_close(h int)` marks handle h not live and, when `sz_cf` is a folder of h, sets `sz_cf` to -1 and clears `sz_cbuf`; then, while the last `SzArc` in `sz_arcs` is not live, it calls `sz_undo` to cut every list back to that archive's start (its index for `sz_arcs`, e0, n0, f0, c0, p0, o0, r0 and base for the others). A bad or already closed handle does nothing.
